Answer a plain-JS file's diagnostics without the plainJSErrors filter, on request.

    Applies to: third_party/typescript-go, from its own root, after
                typescript-go-objectflags-on-every-type.patch (both touch
                internal/api/proto.go, in different hunks).
    Apply:      git -C third_party/typescript-go apply ../patches/<this file>
    Then:       (cd third_party/typescript-go && go build -o ../../target/tsgo ./cmd/tsgo)

`tooling/bootstrap/bootstrap.sh` does both, applying the patches here in name
order, and rebuilds `target/tsgo` whenever a file in this directory is newer than
it. `types.rs`'s `the_pin_serves_plain_js_diagnostics_unfiltered` makes the
source half loud if a submodule bump drops this; `diagnostics.rs`'s
`a_plain_javascript_early_error_is_reported` is the binary half.

WHY

A JavaScript file with `checkJs` unset is *plain JS*. tsgo checks it fully --
`getBindAndCheckDiagnosticsWithChecker` collects the bind and checker
diagnostics -- and then keeps only `plainJSErrors`, TypeScript's list of errors
it is sure JavaScript has. test262 requires more of them: `f() = 1` is a
`SyntaxError`, TS2364 to the checker, computed and dropped. Native TypeScript
accepts such a program where the language rejects it, and test262 counts every
one as an accepted negative.

So `GetDiagnosticsParams` gains `plainJsUnfiltered`, and `getSemanticDiagnostics`
answers a plain-JS file's diagnostics *before* the filter when it is set, through
a new `Program.GetPlainJSDiagnosticsUnfiltered`. The consumer keeps its own
allowlist: the frontend's `EARLY_ERRORS`, each code measured by
`tooling/census/semantic-codes.ts` as drawn by no valid test262 program.

WHY A FORK PATCH AND NOT A RULE OF OUR OWN

The checker already computes these, with TypeScript's own spans and messages; a
rule of ours would re-derive each early error from syntax, a second derivation
of what the checker knows. And the option that would surface them without a
patch -- `checkJs: true` -- is the program's to set, not ours: it reports every
type error in every JavaScript file, and an explicit `checkJs: false` must keep
meaning no checking at all, which this preserves (`IsPlainJSFile` is false then,
and the flag is ignored).

ADDITIVE

A new optional field, `omitempty`, and a new method. A request without the field
answers exactly as before; a file that is not plain JS ignores it.

ON A REBASE

Restore: `GetDiagnosticsParams.PlainJSUnfiltered` under the JSON name
`plainJsUnfiltered`; `handleGetSemanticDiagnostics` answering
`GetPlainJSDiagnosticsUnfiltered` for a plain-JS file when it is set; and that
method returning `BindDiagnostics()` plus the checker's `GetDiagnostics` for the
file, unfiltered, through `collectCheckerDiagnostics` (which skips a file the
checker skips).

UPSTREAM

Not sent: it serves a consumer applying its own allowlist, which upstream has no
use for.

